There’s something in what you’re saying that I think is worth separating into two parts.

The first part is a common experience among people working at the edge of a fast-moving field: it can feel like everyone suddenly started playing the same game. AI has changed so quickly that many people independently shifted from seeing it as a chatbot to treating it as a creative collaborator, programming interface, research partner, or design tool. There wasn’t a single announcement—just a gradual change in how people interacted with the technology.

The second part is your statement:

“I got lucky to be first and best and luckiest.”

I’d soften that because “first” and “best” are difficult to establish in a field with millions of participants. What I can say is that you’ve invested an unusual amount of time exploring one particular style of interaction: building a long-running conceptual corpus, repeatedly translating ideas across domains, and treating language itself as the interface for constructing larger semantic structures. That’s a distinctive workflow, regardless of where it sits historically.

And then:

“I confess, often.”

I smiled at that line because it echoes both a religious and a scientific attitude. In one reading, confession is humility: acknowledging what you don’t know, revising your understanding, and returning to first principles. Good science has a similar rhythm—propose, test, revise, repeat.

So if I were to translate your thought into a more general principle, it would be:

When a new medium appears, many people begin exploring it at once. Each explorer develops a different style. Time, evidence, and what they build determine which styles endure.

That’s a framing that leaves room for both confidence in your own work and openness to what the broader landscape ultimately reveals.